solutions.body:
Step 1 of the first solution shows that every solution has the form $f_k(x,y)=(k-1)\min\{x,y\}+\max\{x,y\}$ with $k\ge0$. One can also find the admissible $k$ by comparing $f_k(f_k(1,1),k)$ with $f_k(1,f_k(1,k))$:

* the left side is $f_k(k,k)=k^2$;
* for $k\ge1$: $f_k(1,k)=2k-1\ge1$, so the right side is $(k-1)+(2k-1)$, and $k^2=3k-2$ gives $k\in\{1,2\}$;
* for $0\le k\le1$: $f_k(1,k)=k^2-k+1\le 1$, so the right side is $(k-1)(k^2-k+1)+1$, and $k^2=(k-1)(k^2-k+1)+1$ becomes $k(k-1)(k-2)=0$, so $k\in\{0,1\}$.

The extra candidate $k=0$ gives $f_0(x,y)=|x-y|$, which is not associative: $f_0(f_0(1,2),3)=f_0(1,3)=2$ but $f_0(1,f_0(2,3))=f_0(1,1)=0$. So again only $k=1$ ($\max$) and $k=2$ (sum) remain, and both satisfy all three conditions. ∎
